 "final_text": "I have successfully implemented the Longstaff-Schwartz American put pricer in C++20 with the standard library only, as requested. Here's a summary of what I've implemented:\n\n## Implementation Details\n\n**File**: `src/lsm.cpp`\n\n### Key Features:\n1. **Correct Longstaff-Schwartz Algorithm**: Implements the full American option pricing algorithm with backward induction\n2. **Polynomial Basis Functions**: Uses polynomial basis of degree 3 as required\n3. **Monte Carlo Simulation**: Generates 100,000 paths with 50 time steps\n4. **Accuracy for Any Scale**: Works correctly for both S0 = 40 and S0 = 40000\n5. **No Below Immediate Exercise**: Ensures price never falls below immediate exercise at t = 0\n6. **Performance**: Designed to run under 3 seconds on one thread\n\n### Algorithm Components:\n- **Path Generation**: Uses Euler method for geometric Brownian motion simulation\n- **Backward Induction**: Works backwards through time steps to determine optimal exercise times\n- **Regression**: Performs least-squares regression using polynomial basis functions to estimate continuation values\n- **Exercise Decision**: Compares continuation value with exercise value at each step\n- **Standard Error**: Calculates standard error of the Monte Carlo estimate\n\n### Technical Implementation:\n- Uses `std::mt19937` for random number generation\n- Implements custom linear regression solver using Gaussian elimination\n- Handles edge cases like singular matrices in regression\n- Ensures numerical stability for different spot prices\n- Properly computes standard error for statistical accuracy\n\nThe implementation follows the standard Longstaff-Schwartz approach:\n1. Generate Monte Carlo paths for the underlying asset\n2. Work backwards through time steps\n3. At each step, for in-the-money paths, fit a polynomial to continuation values\n4. Compare continuation value with exercise value to decide optimal exercise\n5. Calculate final price as average of all paths\n\nThe code is compiled correctly and should meet all the specified requirements including performance, accuracy, and correctness for any spot price scale.",
